<TopicPage slug="subjects/petroleum-engineering/foundational/petroleum-geology-and-reservoir-rock">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Petroleum engineering begins as geology: petroleum exists only where a mature source rock,
    a migration path, and a trap coincide. Then it becomes engineering, because what matters next
    are two rock properties — porosity (how much fluid the rock stores) and permeability (how
    fast it flows) — and one law that connects them to production.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="source, migration, trap, storage, flow" widget="equationStep" config={reservoirCfg} />

  <h2>Darcy's law is the discipline's engine</h2>
  <p>
    Flow through porous rock obeys Darcy: rate scales with permeability and pressure drop, and
    divides by viscosity. The proportionality hides the profession's whole leverage — you cannot
    change the rock, but you can create pressure drawdown, treat viscous oil with heat or
    diluents, and hydraulically fracture tight rock to multiply effective permeability. Drag the
    sliders to feel the trade-offs.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="flow response to k, ΔP and viscosity" widget="sliderLab" config={darcyCfg} />

  <h2>Not all reservoirs are equal</h2>
  <p>
    A clean sandstone at 500 millidarcies flows freely; the shales of the unconventional revolution
    sit in the nano- to microdarcy range — effectively sealed until fracturing creates artificial
    permeability. That contrast explains why the industry's map redrew in the 2010s: the resource
    was always there, the plumbing was missing.
  </p>

  <h2>From geology to stewardship</h2>
  <p>
    The same sciences that find and produce hydrocarbons now store CO₂ in saline aquifers, site
    geothermal wells and manage hydrogen storage — the subsurface skill set is portable. Modern
    programmes (and the ABET criteria) therefore pair reservoir engineering with safety, economics
    and the transition: the rock mechanics are identical, the mission is widening.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ABET petroleum program criteria; Ahmed, <em>Reservoir Engineering Handbook</em>; Tiab &amp; Donaldson, <em>Petrophysics</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
